window.METHODS=[`<h3>三段不同的过程</h3><p><b>训练改变模型权重。</b>本项目在 PathMNIST 结直肠组织显微图的固定子集上，对 ImageNet MobileNet V1 0.25 的 conv_pw_11 与新分类头做了 8 轮监督微调。它是小规模实验，不代表完整器官识别能力。conv_pw_5 在这次微调中冻结，因此该层的基础／微调响应应一致。</p><p><b>查看响应固定图片与模型。</b>热图来自所选模型、层和通道的真实前向输出。每图用自身 min–max 归一化到 [0,1]；常量响应映射为 0。颜色表示相对响应，不表示器官位置；跨模型同编号通道不保证相同语义。</p><p><b>Dream 图像优化改变图片像素。</b>最大化选定通道或整层的平均激活；梯度除以 RMS，以所选步长更新 [-1,1] 像素。只保存每 2 步及最后一步的真实帧，无插值。生成中改参数将取消并从原图重启。</p><h3>实验范围与实现</h3><p>训练数据为 900 张、验证 225 张、测试 225 张，按官方划分分别每类抽样；随机种子 5316。28×28 图像放大到 64×64，仅为适配特征提取，不增加细节。训练曲线来自真实日志；测试集仅在最后评估。检查点 0 / 2 / 4 / 8 保存权重与 SHA-256。短实验的结果不等于泛化到人体器官、日常照片或医疗场景。</p><p>训练由本地 Node / TensorFlow.js CPU 离线执行；页面加载真实检查点，在浏览器执行前向响应和图像优化，无需长时托管后端。上传照片仅在浏览器内处理；缩小到最长边 256 像素、不裁剪，透明区域使用白底。生成版本与原图、过程和配置一起记录。模型文件、记录和案例均随本地网站提供，断外网仍可使用。远程网址不承诺断网重载。</p><h3>Scientist / Artist</h3><p>Mordvintsev、Olah 与 Tyka 的 Inceptionism 研究展示特征可视化与 DeepDream。Memo Akten《Learning to See: Interactive》（2017）使用摄像头与定制模型重构画面；本项目增强响应，两者机制不同。可共同讨论“训练经验如何影响所见”。“梦”是比喻，不证明机器具有主观体验。</p><h3>来源与许可</h3><p>PathMNIST：Jiancheng Yang 等，MedMNIST v2（2023）；原始组织图来自 Jakob Nikolas Kather 等，NCT-CRC-HE-100K / CRC-VAL-HE-7K（2019），CC BY 4.0。此项目进行了固定子集抽样、尺寸调整、微调与可视化。NASA / Eileen Collins 照片为公共领域；咖啡照片由 Rachel Michetti 提供、猫照片由 Stefan van der Walt 提供，均为 CC0，via scikit-image。</p><p>技术案例和候选预设未冒充小组共同策划的最终作品。小组仍需补齐自摄照片、选择理由、双 hero 分析、Miro 展陈证据与课程 PDF。</p>`,
`<h3>Three distinct processes</h3><p><b>Training changes model weights.</b>We ran 8 supervised epochs on a fixed subset of PathMNIST colorectal tissue microscopy, fine-tuning ImageNet MobileNet V1 0.25 conv_pw_11 and a new classifier. This is a small pilot, not a complete organ-recognition model. conv_pw_5 remains frozen, so its base and fine-tuned responses should match.</p><p><b>Activation inspection fixes both image and model.</b>Maps are actual forward outputs of the selected model, layer and channel. Each map is independently min–max normalized to [0,1]; constant maps become 0. Colors show relative responses, not organ locations. Matching channel indices across models do not guarantee matching semantics.</p><p><b>Dream optimization changes image pixels.</b>Maximize the mean activation of a selected channel or whole layer, normalize gradients by RMS and update pixels in [-1,1] using the chosen step size. Real frames are saved every 2 updates and at completion, with no interpolation. Parameter edits cancel and restart from the original image.</p><h3>Scope & implementation</h3><p>900 training, 225 validation and 225 test images are sampled by class from their respective official splits, using seed 5316. Upscaling 28×28 images to 64×64 supports feature extraction but adds no detail. Curves come from actual logs; the test split is evaluated only at the end. Epoch 0 / 2 / 4 / 8 checkpoints include weights and SHA-256 hashes. These limited results do not establish generalization to organs, everyday photographs or medical use.</p><p>Training runs offline in local Node / TensorFlow.js CPU. The site loads real checkpoints and computes activations and Dreams in the browser, without a long-running hosted backend. Uploaded images remain in the browser; longest side is capped at 256 pixels without cropping, with transparency composited on white. Model versions, input, frames and settings are recorded together. Local files include models and recorded runs for offline use; reloading the remote URL offline is not guaranteed.</p><h3>Scientist / Artist</h3><p>Inceptionism by Mordvintsev, Olah and Tyka demonstrates feature visualization and DeepDream. Memo Akten’s Learning to See: Interactive (2017) reconstructs camera input with custom models; this project amplifies responses. The mechanisms differ, while both invite discussion of how training experience affects seeing. “Dream” is a metaphor, not evidence of subjective experience.</p><h3>Sources & licenses</h3><p>PathMNIST: Jiancheng Yang et al., MedMNIST v2 (2023); source histology by Jakob Nikolas Kather et al., NCT-CRC-HE-100K / CRC-VAL-HE-7K (2019), CC BY 4.0. This project subsamples, resizes, fine-tunes and visualizes the data. The NASA Eileen Collins photograph is public domain. Coffee by Rachel Michetti and Chelsea the cat by Stefan van der Walt are CC0, via scikit-image.</p><p>Technical examples and candidate presets are not presented as a group-curated final artifact. Group photographs, curation notes, dual-hero analysis, Miro evidence and the course PDF still require group input.</p>`];
window.METHOD_LINKS=`<ul><li><a href="https://www.tensorflow.org/tutorials/generative/deepdream" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">TensorFlow — DeepDream</a></li><li><a href="https://research.google/blog/inceptionism-going-deeper-into-neural-networks/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google Research — Inceptionism (2015)</a></li><li><a href="https://memo.tv/projects/2017/learning-to-see-interactive/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Memo Akten — Learning to See: Interactive (2017)</a></li><li><a href="https://zenodo.org/records/10519652" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MedMNIST / PathMNIST — CC BY 4.0</a></li><li><a href="https://scikit-image.org/docs/stable/api/skimage.data.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">scikit-image — photo attribution</a></li><li><a href="vendor/TENSORFLOW_LICENSE.txt" target="_blank">TensorFlow.js / MobileNet — Apache 2.0</a></li></ul>`;
